'''Johari window''' is a four-quadrant heuristic for discussing interpersonal awareness: which information about a person is known or unknown to that person and to other people. Joseph Luft and Harrington Ingham introduced the model in 1955.<ref name="luft1955">Luft, Joseph, and Harrington Ingham (1955). ''The Johari Window: A Graphic Model of Interpersonal Awareness''. Proceedings of the Western Training Laboratory in Group Development, UCLA, Los Angeles. Bibliographic record: https://bibbase.org/network/publication/ingham-luft-thejohariwindowagraphicmodelofinterpersonalawareness-1955</ref>

{{:Main/Johari window/Simple}}

==Scope==
The window is a framework for reflection and discussion, not a validated division of personality into four immutable components. The meaning of what is “known” depends on the person, audience, context and time. It is distinct from a psychometric test and does not establish the effectiveness of any particular training intervention.<ref name="apa">American Psychological Association, ''APA Dictionary of Psychology'', “Johari window” (updated 19 April 2018). https://dictionary.apa.org/johari-window</ref>

